\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Which symmetry selectors restrict}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let a finite group #{\Gamma} act orthogonally on a
finite-dimensional real Euclidean space #{H}, and let
#{Q>0} be self-adjoint and #{\Gamma}-equivariant. Two constructions
of [symmetry selectors](fdip-005A) have different criteria.}
\ol{
\li{For a self-adjoint idempotent #{e\in\mathbb R[\Gamma]},
the selector #{P=\rho(e)} restricts whenever its rank is nonzero
and proper. Its image need not be a #{\Gamma}-subrepresentation.}
\li{A nonzero proper #{\Gamma}-subrepresentation #{X} restricts precisely when
its multiplicity subspace in every real isotypic block is invariant
under the corresponding Hermitian precision matrix #{Q_\Xi}.}
}

\p{Here is the precise multiplicity convention. Use the
[real commutant](fdip-004Z) and the
[right-scalar column coordinates](fdip-0050). For
#{D_\Xi=\operatorname{End}_\Gamma(W_\Xi)} and
#{r_\Xi=\dim_{D_\Xi}W_\Xi}, represent #{H_\Xi} by
#{r_\Xi} columns in #{D_\Xi^{m_\Xi}}; matrices act on each
column by left multiplication. There is a right #{D_\Xi}-subspace
#{U_\Xi\subseteq D_\Xi^{m_\Xi}} such that
##{X\cap H_\Xi
 \cong\{(z_1,\ldots,z_{r_\Xi}):z_a\in U_\Xi\}.}
The criterion is
##{Q_\Xi U_\Xi\subseteq U_\Xi\qquad\text{for every }\Xi.}
Equivalently, each #{U_\Xi} has a #{D_\Xi}-basis of
#{Q_\Xi}-eigenvectors. Repeated eigenvalues allow proper subspaces
of an eigenspace; whole eigenspaces need not be selected.}

\proof{
\p{Equivariance makes #{Q} commute with every #{\rho(g)}, hence
with #{\rho(e)}. The [restriction criterion](fdip-005M) proves
the first assertion. Centrality is not needed for this commutation;
it would additionally make #{\rho(e)} commute with the group action.}

\p{For a subrepresentation #{X}, both #{X} and #{X^\perp} are
#{\Gamma}-stable, so its orthogonal projector belongs to the
commutant. By \ref{fdip-004Z} it is represented by Hermitian
idempotents #{p_\Xi\in M_{m_\Xi}(D_\Xi)}. In the stated column
coordinates its image has exactly the displayed form with
#{U_\Xi=\operatorname{im}p_\Xi}. Conversely a right
#{D_\Xi}-subspace has an orthogonal projector commuting with
right scalar multiplication: its real orthogonal complement is
right-scalar stable, since multiplication by each unit scalar is
orthogonal and preserves the subspace. Thus that projector is a
matrix over #{D_\Xi}; the commutant identification gives the
corresponding subrepresentation.}

\p{The matrix #{Q_\Xi} acts on every column, so #{QX\subseteq X}
is exactly the displayed invariance condition. If it holds,
self-adjointness preserves the orthogonal complement too. The
Hermitian spectral argument of \ref{fdip-0050} applies to the
restriction on #{U_\Xi}: a real eigenvector generates a right
#{D_\Xi}-line of eigenvectors, its orthogonal complement is
invariant, and induction gives a #{D_\Xi}-orthonormal eigenbasis.
Conversely a span of eigenvectors is invariant, since their
eigenvalues are real and the matrix is right-linear. Apply
\ref{fdip-005M} to finish.}
}

\p{In a multiplicity-free representation every
#{m_\Xi=1}, and a right #{D_\Xi}-subspace of #{D_\Xi} is either
zero or all of it. Thus every nonzero proper subrepresentation restricts. At
higher multiplicity, group stability alone does not suffice, as
the [prism line](fdip-005O) shows.}
